      "writtenOn": "2026-09-29T16:55:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Clearing `[__ahb4_start, __ahb4_end)` only in `system_pre_init()` does not fully address b/524097509:\n\n1. **Field devices with write-protected RO:** On deployed devices (`dartmonkey`, `nocturne_fp`, `nami_fp`), RO is hardware write-protected (`WP_ON`) and will not receive this update; only RW is updated. If the EC resets from RW and remains in RO (without jumping to RW), RW\u0027s `system_pre_init()` never runs after the reset, and factory RO will still see stale RW `.ahb4` contents. To protect field devices, RW also needs to clear `.ahb4` before resetting in `system_reset()` (e.g., right after `cpu_disable_caches()`).\n2. **RO vs. RW `.ahb4` size mismatch:** Even when RO is updated, `memset(__ahb4_start, 0, (uintptr_t)__ahb4_end - (uintptr_t)__ahb4_start)` only clears the `.ahb4` range of the *currently booting* image. In RO, `.ahb4` is only ~2.1 KB (`tx_buf` + `rx_buf`), whereas in RW `.ahb4` is \u003e40 KB (`fp_buffer`, `ctx`, `spi_buf`, `tx_buf`, `rx_buf`). When resetting from RW and staying in RO, ~95% of RW\u0027s `.ahb4` region remains uncleared in SRAM4.\n3. **`.ahb` (`NOLOAD`) region:** On `nocturne_fp`/`dartmonkey`, `fp_template` and `fp_enc_buffer` are placed in `.ahb` (`0x30000000`, D2 SRAM1-3 in `chip/stm32/memory_regions.inc`), which is also `NOLOAD` and persists across resets. Please check whether `.ahb` (`[__ahb_start, __ahb_end)`) should also be cleared (note that D2 SRAM1-3 clocks in `RCC_AHB2ENR` bits 29-31 must be enabled before accessing `0x30000000`, whereas D3 SRAM4 in `RCC_AHB4ENR` bit 29 is enabled by default out of reset).",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 306,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 312,
        "endChar": 9999
      },
      "isAi": true,
      "revId": "c547b871ee795c0df06d16296a1860c9fe5fd15a",
